WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.420
MARK MIRCHANDANI: The Google
Cloud Talent Solution Job

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:04.440
Search API uses machine
learning to determine

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.778
the relevance of job listings
when a user searches for jobs.

00:00:07.778 --> 00:00:10.320
Let's talk a bit more about what
determines search relevance.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.808
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.808 --> 00:00:17.100
If you haven't seen the
previous videos in this series,

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.200
I definitely recommend
checking them out.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.140
Jobs in companies have
quite a few properties

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.890
that the machine
learning models reference

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.170
to determine the relevance
of search results.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.570
You can influence that
relevance significantly

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:30.210
by providing more
information, or using things

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.759
like custom ranking
and featured jobs.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.380
Of course, search
result relevance

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.480
can be complex to
measure, especially

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.190
since different things are
relevant to different people.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.280
The job search API
uses an algorithm

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.920
based on a few signals
from the job data.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.420
One of the most
important properties

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.370
is geographic location.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.050
Both companies and jobs
have location properties,

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:53.370
but the job location will be
used over the company location

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.080
when a user does a search.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.910
You should be using
the specific address

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.985
fields for jobs and
companies so that the API can

00:00:59.985 --> 00:01:02.370
geolocate it and use
it to return the most

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.470
relevant jobs for a search.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.690
If someone searches
for San Francisco,

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.130
they probably want to see jobs
in and around San Francisco

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:10.530
first and foremost.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.560
The next determination
is how fresh a job is.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.660
Basically, newer jobs
are ranked higher

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.580
than updated or older jobs.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.260
Like all of these
signals, it's used

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.720
in combination with the
data to make sure the search

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:22.990
results are relevant.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.710
So a newer job that's
completely unrelated

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.770
shouldn't show up at the
top just because it's new.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.270
The title and
description of a job

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.760
play a major role in determining
if it's right for a search

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.150
query, of course.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.540
If a job for a scrum
master is posted

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.480
and the description mentions
managing software developers,

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.850
then a search for "software
development manager"

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.660
should include that job.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.900
Make sure to avoid just
stuffing a bunch of keywords

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.210
into a job description,
or you may end up

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:51.150
with some less relevant
jobs being surfaced.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.340
The API also has some
parameters that you

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.850
can pass when doing a search
to better control the results.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.280
Keyword match lets
you enable or disable

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.130
if the search should look for
keywords beyond the regular ML

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.810
driven search results.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:07.170
Broadening will widen the search
scope for the given parameters,

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.870
allowing you to get more
results for a search.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.000
Diversification
level lets you choose

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.370
if similar jobs should appear
near each other in search

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:18.090
results, or if it should only
display the most relevant one.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.520
Check out the earlier
Best Practices video

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.012
to see some more
details on these.

00:02:23.012 --> 00:02:24.720
There's also a lot of
features of the API

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.870
to consider when searching,
such as any filters of type

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.630
like employment, or
distance from a location.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.670
Using the commute
search options lets

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:34.380
you filter by how
long it would take

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.900
to travel to and from the job.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.180
Using featured jobs lets
you promote individual jobs.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.640
So they could appear in
their own promoted section.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.400
You can even enable
custom ranking

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.620
to manually influence
search results based

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.383
on your different custom fields.

00:02:48.383 --> 00:02:49.800
With all of these
factors in play,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.600
the API has a lot
of considerations

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.720
when determining which jobs
are most relevant to a search.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.560
Your business logic may also
dictate certain requirements,

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:00.150
which you can achieve through
the search parameters and API

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.170
features.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.990
In addition, the ML
models will tune over time

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:05.940
if you've hooked up
the API events as users

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:07.780
interact with your job search.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.770
So if you're seeing results that
are unexpected or surprising

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.200
when doing a search,
here's a quick checklist

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.780
to help investigate
what might be happening.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:19.170
First up, if a job isn't showing
up when you think it should be,

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.820
make sure to check the details.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.500
With all the factors
we just mentioned,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.960
it's important to double
check the title, description,

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.990
location, and any of the filters
that may be on the search that

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:29.250
might exclude it.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.280
Also, check to see that
the job hasn't expired yet

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.040
since expired jobs won't
return in the results.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.872
If the search request has
diversification level enabled,

00:03:37.872 --> 00:03:39.330
the job you're
looking for may have

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.190
been moved to the
end of the list,

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:42.960
because a more relevant
and very similar

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.280
job was displayed first.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.500
On the other hand, if a job
is showing up in results

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.680
that it shouldn't
be, you should still

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.630
start to check with
the job details.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.250
When checking for
details, make sure

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.120
to look at the
location for the job,

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.330
and if the search request
may include a location,

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:00.240
since that may push this
job higher up in relevance.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.820
Additionally, check to see if
the search request has enabled

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.190
broadening turned
on, which will relax

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:09.720
some of the stricter job search
conditions to return more jobs.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:11.803
If a job is showing up
lower in the search results

00:04:11.803 --> 00:04:13.470
than you would expect,
you'll definitely

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.450
want to check everything
on the search request.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.790
Custom ranking, feature
jobs, keyword matching,

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.070
and diversification
level can all heavily

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.500
affect the order in
which jobs show up.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.900
You should also look at
the location of the search

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.440
and at the more relevant jobs to
see if they more closely align.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.590
Also, don't forget to check the
freshness of the job posting

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:35.280
to see if the more relevant
jobs are also more recent.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.110
If the locations for
a job are showing up

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.300
incorrectly or in
unexpected places,

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.630
check the derived location
information for a job.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:45.480
The information provided for the
job location may have targeted

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.460
the job somewhere
else than intended,

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.920
or the job may be
inheriting its location

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:51.450
from the parent company.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:52.950
Make sure you're
using the Addresses

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.470
field with a full address
rather than putting

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:57.660
the address in the
description or putting

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:00.930
something not specific
enough, like just California.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.000
If a job isn't returning
for some searches that

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.310
use industry-specific
terms, it's

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.230
most likely that those
terms aren't recognized

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:08.970
by the Job Search API.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:10.740
If you've hooked
up events handling,

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:13.230
the ML models will train
themselves over time

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.580
to better match searches
for those words.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.640
In addition, you can
use custom fields

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.260
to include synonyms
and other search

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.063
values to help more jobs show up
when an industry term is used.

00:05:23.063 --> 00:05:24.480
These are some
steps you can check

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.810
as you integrate and debug
the implementation of the API.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:29.710
If you're still not
sure what's going on,

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:32.630
make sure to grab the request ID
from your search request, which

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.860
is returned with
every API request.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.440
That ID has all the
information for the search,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.570
and you can provide
it to the support team

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.640
so that they can help
figure out what's going on

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.410
if you file a support request.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:46.685
As you can see, there's a lot
of power in the Job Search API.

00:05:46.685 --> 00:05:48.060
If you haven't
already, check out

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.760
our previous videos and the
documentation for a deeper

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.177
dive on these subjects.

00:05:52.177 --> 00:05:53.010
Thanks for watching.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:54.840
And remember, when
looking for talent,

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.790
it's OK to keep your
head in the Cloud.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:06:00.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

